**Proposition 7.2 (credited endpoint attainment and nonattainment facts).**
At $\tau=1$ the primal is finitely attained, its value is
$j(1)=\operatorname{tr}G-B/2$, and the original value is $T_0$.
The endpoint dual infimum equals $j(1)$ but has no finite minimizer.
These JR51 endpoint facts coexist with the open-branch finite
certificates and divergent normalized multiplier.

Proof. Null-decompose an endpoint tuple. The signed coordinate obeys

$$
\sum r f_0f_1\leq\frac12\sum(f_0^2+f_1^2)=1.
\tag{EP.47}
$$

Equality forces $f_1=rf_0$ for each nonzero atom, and nullity gives
$f_2^2=f_0^2$. The four corner rays are therefore exhaustive. Their
Gram normalization forces mass $1/4$ on each unscaled
$(1,\sigma,\eta)$ ray, from the total mass and three zero mixed
moments. The three positive and one negative corner heights in EP.18
give the stated value. Section 8 at the four corners provides a finite
actual common instrument, rather than only a PSD witness.

For $\tau<1$, mix an endpoint feasible tuple with $I/4$ in all four
slots, using endpoint weight $a\in(\tau,1)$. Every slot is PD, every
null trace is zero and contrast is $a>\tau$. Finite-dimensional Slater
duality gives finite dual attainment. Compactness and nested feasible
sets give $j(\tau)\to j(1)$ as $\tau\uparrow1$: every subsequential
limit of optimizers is endpoint feasible. An interior dual optimum
remains endpoint-dual feasible with objective
$j(\tau)-\lambda(1-\tau)\leq j(\tau)$. Endpoint weak duality
sandwiches its dual infimum between $j(1)$ and these converging upper
values, proving equality.

For an independent algebraic check of no finite minimum, suppose such
a minimum existed. Complementarity with the two positive corners having
$r=+$ forces $S_{+,+}=a kk^T$, $k=(1,-1,0)^T$, $a\geq0$.
Writing $\nu_0=\nu_{+,+}$ gives
$Y=G+\lambda C-\nu_0Q+a kk^T$. The positive corner $(1,-1,1)$ in
the other positive-height block gives

$$
(\nu_{-,+}-\nu_0)Q(1,-1,1)^T+(2a-\lambda)k=0.
\tag{EP.48}
$$

Its third coordinate forces $\nu_{-,+}=\nu_0$, hence $2a=\lambda$.
The negative corner $(1,-1,-1)$ then gives
$\nu_{-,-}-\nu_0=2(x+y-g_0)$ and requires $R_1=R_2=0$.
EP.18 already makes both strictly positive. For example its weaker
rational consequences imply
$R_1>.00237-.00293+.00010+.87(.00330)>0$ and
$R_2>.00220+.00010-.00168+.12(.00330)>0$.
This contradiction does not extrapolate interior Slater attainment.
Alternatively EP.9's positive square-root gain rules out a finite
endpoint supporting slope. No native impossibility or failure of finite
primal attainment follows. $\square$

